  "mechanismSummary": "KHMDS deprotonates the sulfone next to sulfur, and the anion adds to benzaldehyde. The alkoxide attacks the electron-poor tetrazole carbon (a spiro intermediate), and the tetrazole moves from sulfur to oxygen: a Smiles rearrangement. The sulfinate then loses SO₂ while the tetrazolyloxide leaves from the next carbon, forming the alkene, mainly E (not checked by the app yet). The classical Julia needs a separate acylation and a sodium amalgam reduction; this version does it in one pot.",
